      "text": "The integer part of the square root of a number is the greatest unique integer less than or equal to $\\sqrt{x}$.\nFor example, $\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor = 1$ and $\\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor = 3,$ and we see that $\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = 4$.\n\nThe sum of the roots of \n\\[\\lfloor \\sqrt{x} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{x+1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{x+2} \\rfloor = ((x+1)+(x+2))/2\\]can be approximated by the sum of intervals\n\\[*  \\begin{align*}\n& \\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor &= 1+1=2 & (1,2] \\\\\n& \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor &= 1+2+2=5 & (2,4] \\\\\n& \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor+ \\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor &= 2+2+3+2=9 &(3,9]\n\\end{align*}\\]\nand then estimating the sum of the intervals:\n\\[\\sum_{i=1}^{2} (9-i+1) = 20.\\]\nTherefore, the sum of the in intervals is very close, so the sum of floors we are after is\n\\[2 + 5 + 9 + 20 - 2 = \\boxed{34}.\\]The answer is: 34",
